---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import config from '../../../site.config';
import { absoluteUrl, formatShortDate, getEventsForProgramme, getProgrammes, paths } from '../../lib/site';
import { ogPathFor } from '../../lib/og';
import { collectionPageSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const programmes = await getProgrammes();

const rows = await Promise.all(
  programmes.map(async (programme) => ({
    programme,
    ...(await getEventsForProgramme(programme.id)),
  })),
);

/*
 * The meta description says what the three days are and when the next ones run,
 * and derives the dates rather than stating them.
 *
 * This is the hub that lists every programme, and its description did neither. It
 * read "repeating, in-person, hands-on formats for building with agentic AI",
 * which names no date and describes one line of the three: the Second Brain
 * Bootcamp is not agentic-workflow building, and it is on this page. That is
 * backlog 14's fault class on a seventh surface. The dates belong here for the
 * reason they were added to /events/ - the description is the field an answer
 * engine quotes most readily, and the next cohort's date is already published in
 * the header and footer of every page, so nothing new is asserted. The 29 Oct
 * probe is the evidence: an engine asked for a one-day hands-on AI bootcamp in
 * Oxford in November 2026 answered off a third party's July listing of ours and
 * concluded there was none.
 *
 * `formatsLine` is the one hand-written part, so it is guarded by the programme
 * count: a fourth line falls back to the generic sentence rather than silently
 * describing three of four. The dates are capped at the next two and dropped one
 * at a time if the pair would pass the 160 characters a description gets.
 */
const nextDates = rows
  .flatMap(({ upcoming }) => upcoming)
  .sort((a, b) => a.data.startDate.getTime() - b.data.startDate.getTime())
  .slice(0, 2)
  .map((event) => formatShortDate(event.data.startDate));

const formatsLine =
  programmes.length === 3
    ? 'Three one-day bootcamps in Oxford, hands-on and in person: build an agentic workflow, a second brain, or ship software.'
    : `Every one-day bootcamp ${config.brand.name} runs in Oxford, hands-on and in person, with what each day covers.`;

let description = formatsLine;
for (let count = nextDates.length; count > 0; count -= 1) {
  const dates = nextDates.slice(0, count);
  const candidate = `${formatsLine} Next cohort${dates.length > 1 ? 's' : ''}: ${dates.join(', ')}.`;
  if (candidate.length <= 160) {
    description = candidate;
    break;
  }
}
---

<Base
  {/*
   * The machine title, not the nav label. `pageTitle` is the only thing
   * `Base.astro` composes a <title> from (src/layouts/Base.astro:50), so it also
   * sets og:title, twitter:title and `WebPage.name` - four surfaces. This page
   * passed "Bootcamps", which is the PRD §11 nav label and names nothing a buyer
   * types: no "AI", no format, and "Oxford" only inside the brand suffix. That is
   * the defect the 15 November cycle fixed on /testimonials/ ("Outcomes" ->
   * "What attendees said"), on the page where someone picks a programme.
   *
   * /events/ is the model, and it is deliberately not copied word for word:
   * it holds "AI bootcamp dates in Oxford" because its job is when, and this
   * page's job is which. No count, for the same reason `formatsLine` below is
   * guarded by `programmes.length === 3` - a fourth line must not make the
   * title false. "One-day" is the differentiator a four-week programme cannot
   * claim and the modifier the buyer queries in the GEO log keep using.
   *
   * The nav label, the breadcrumb crumb and the hero h1 ("The programmes") are
   * all left reading "Bootcamps" / their own words - /events/ keeps its own
   * short crumb beside a buyer title, so this matches the shape already on the
   * site. One word puts it back. The `CollectionPage` node carried the short
   * label too until 23 Nov 2026, when it was merged into this page's `WebPage`
   * node under one `@id` and its second name was dropped; see the note on
   * `collectionPageSchema`. The crumb is where that label lives now.
   */}
  pageTitle="One-day AI bootcamps in Oxford"
  description={description}
  path={paths.programmes()}
  ogImage={ogPathFor(paths.programmes())}
  schemaNodes={[
    collectionPageSchema(
      paths.programmes(),
      /*
       * What this page lists is a Course, and the list said so nowhere. Each
       * entry was a bare `ListItem` with a name and a URL, so a consumer that
       * reads this page alone learned that three pages exist and nothing about
       * what they are - while each of the three publishes a full `Course` node
       * of its own, with `teaches`, `audience`, `coursePrerequisites` and a
       * dated `hasCourseInstance`, under `<url>#course`.
       *
       * This is the join the 26 October cycle made on the guides, where
       * `Article.about` named the programme as an anonymous `Thing` with no
       * `@id`, and the one `/events/` has carried since 23 October: a listing
       * whose items are entities in their own right names them as such.
       * `/guides/` and `/team/` stay bare on purpose - an `Article` node here
       * has no `@id` to point at, and the `/team/` question is backlog 24's,
       * which is Drummond's.
       *
       * Identity only, exactly as on the guides. `name` and `url` are the two
       * strings the `ListItem` already published, and the `@id` is derived from
       * the same path the entry is built from, so nothing is asserted here that
       * the programme's own page does not already publish.
       */
      programmes.map((p) => ({
        name: p.data.name,
        path: paths.programme(p.data.slug),
        item: {
          '@type': 'Course',
          '@id': `${absoluteUrl(paths.programme(p.data.slug))}#course`,
          name: p.data.name,
          url: absoluteUrl(paths.programme(p.data.slug)),
        },
      })),
    ),
  ]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Bootcamps', path: paths.programmes() }]}
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow={config.brand.name}
    title="The programmes"
    subline="Each programme is a repeating format. Cohorts run several times a year, always in person, always in Oxford."
  />

  <section class="band band--cream" aria-labelledby="all">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="all" class="visually-hidden">All programmes</h2>
      <div class="rows">
        {
          rows.map(({ programme, upcoming, past }, i) => (
            <a href={paths.programme(programme.data.slug)} class="card row" data-reveal data-reveal-delay={i * 80}>
              <div>
                <span class="eyebrow">{`0${i + 1}`}</span>
                <h3>{programme.data.name}</h3>
                <p>{programme.data.shortPitch}</p>
                {/* The date, not a count. This is the page where someone picks a
                    programme, and "can I make it?" is half of that decision - every
                    other listing on the site (events index, footer, the programme
                    page itself) names the day, so a bare "1 upcoming" sent them a
                    click further for the one fact they came for. */}
                <p class="row__counts">
                  {upcoming.length === 0 && 'Next dates being confirmed'}
                  {upcoming.length === 1 && (
                    <Fragment>Next cohort <span class="row__nb">{formatShortDate(upcoming[0].data.startDate)}</span></Fragment>
                  )}
                  {upcoming.length > 1 && (
                    <Fragment>{upcoming.length} upcoming, next <span class="row__nb">{formatShortDate(upcoming[0].data.startDate)}</span></Fragment>
                  )}
                  {past.length > 0 && (
                    <Fragment> <span class="row__nb">{`· ${past.length} already run`}</span></Fragment>
                  )}
                </p>
              </div>
              <span class="row__arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                →
              </span>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .rows {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .row h3 {
    margin-block: 0.2rem 0.65rem;
    font-size: var(--step-xl);
  }

  .row p {
    max-width: 54ch;
    font-size: var(--step-sm);
    color: var(--muted-on-cream);
  }

  .row__counts {
    margin-block-start: 0.8rem;
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.11em;
    font-size: var(--step-xs);
    color: var(--rust);
  }

  /* The date and the count each read as one fact, so a line break inside either
     one ("NEXT COHORT 19 / NOV 2026", "CONFIRMED - 1 / ALREADY RUN") splits
     something that should arrive whole. Both now sit in their own span.

     `nowrap` is only safe while the clause fits the column, which is the card
     minus its padding, the 1.5rem gap and the arrow. Measured across 4 browser
     text sizes x 10 widths on the built site: the longest clause is a constant
     7.69em of this row's font size at every one of them (138px at a 16px root,
     173px at 20px, 208px at 24px, 277px at 32px), and the column first reaches
     it at 320px, 360px, 414px and 600px respectively - at or above 19rem in
     every case. So the rule is held behind a rem media query, which is the one
     that scales with the reader's own text preference where a px one would not
     (see the correction recorded as backlog 52). Below it the line keeps the
     word wrapping it has today rather than overflowing the page.

     The separator travels inside the second span rather than before it, so the
     break falls in front of it: it can lead a continuation line but it can
     never end one with nothing after it, which is the stray-mark fault the
     event card and the event hero were corrected for on 31 October. */
  @media (min-width: 19rem) {
    .row__nb {
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
  }

  .row__arrow {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    color: var(--burnt);
    flex: none;
    transition: transform 220ms var(--ease);
  }

  .row:hover .row__arrow {
    transform: translateX(5px);
  }
</style>
